{{ill|Kawanami Gokoku Shrine|ja|川南護国神社}} (川南護国神社{{ill|ja:川南護国神社|en}}) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|en}} located in {{ill|Miyazaki Prefecture|Miyazaki Prefecture|en}}  {{ill|Japan|en}}. <!-- it is not a Beppyo shrine --> It is a {{ill|Gokoku Shrines|Gokoku Shrine|en}}, or a shrine dedicated to war dead.{{Efn|Is this {{ill|WP:BLUE|en}} considering that the shrine is literally named as such?}} Such shrines were made to serve to enshrine the war dead, and they were all considered "branches" of {{ill|Yasukuni Shrine|en}}. They were renamed from {{ill|Gokoku Shrines|lt=Shokonsha|en}} in 1939.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=TAKAYAMA|first=K. PETER|date=1990|title=Enshrinement and Persistency of Japanese Religion|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/23917081|journal=Journal of Church and State|volume=32|issue=3|pages=527–547|issn=0021-969X}}</ref><!-- derived from content I added to {{ill|Gokoku shrines|en}} from an english source -->It is the paratrooper shrine

'''Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine''' is a shrine located in {{ill|10=ja|11=川南町|Kawaminami, Miyazaki|lt=Kawaminami Town|4=en}} , {{ill|10=ja|11=児湯郡|Koyu District, Miyazaki|lt=Koyu|4=en}} [[Miyazaki Prefecture|District, Miyazaki Prefecture]]. The temple grounds are also known as the birthplace of the Army {{ill|5=Fallschirmtruppe_(Kaiserlich_Japanisches_Heer)|10=ja|11=挺進連隊|Airborne Parachute Unit|4=de}}.

== overview ==
The shrine enshrines the spirits of 634 war dead from the former Kawaminami village and approximately 12,000 war dead from the Army Raiding Corps.  This is because the Teishin Shrine, which was established within the grounds of the Army Volunteer Training Department, was burned down by the {{ill|10=ja|11=連合国軍最高司令官総司令部|Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers|lt=GHQ|4=en}} when it occupied Miyazaki after the war, and was therefore enshrined when the Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine was constructed. <ref name=":0" />

In addition, since the temple grounds are the former site of a landing strip for parachute training, a monument marking the birthplace of airborne parachute units has been erected there. Every year, an annual festival is held by the Memorial Association, attended by the town, surviving families, members of the Airborne Veterans Association and the Airborne Comrades Association, and members of the {{ill|10=ja|11=第1空挺団_(陸上自衛隊)|1st Airborne Brigade (Japan)|4=en}} of the Japan {{ill|10=ja|11=陸上自衛隊|Japan Ground Self-Defense Force|4=en}}, the successor to the Army Volunteer Corps.

Although it calls itself [[Gokoku Shrines|a Gokoku Shrine]], it is not included in the list of Gokoku Shrines across the country posted on [[Yasukuni Shrine|the Yasukuni Shrine]] website. (See the section on Gokoku Shrine.)
[[fr:User:Immanuelle/Kawanami Gokoku Shrine]]

