{{ill|Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine|ja|川南護国神社|1=Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine|12=draft}} (川南護国神社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|en}} located in {{ill|Miyazaki Prefecture|en||12=draft}}  {{ill|Japan|en}}.  It is a {{ill|Gokoku Shrines|Gokoku Shrine|en|12=draft}}, or a shrine dedicated to war dead.{{Efn|Is this {{ill|WP:BLUE|en}} considering that the shrine is literally named as such?}} Such shrines were made to serve to enshrine the war dead, and they were all considered "branches" of {{ill|Yasukuni Shrine|en|12=draft|12=draft}}. They were renamed from {{ill|Gokoku Shrines|lt=Shokonsha|en|12=draft}} in 1939.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=TAKAYAMA|first=K. PETER|date=1990|title=Enshrinement and Persistency of Japanese Religion|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/23917081|journal=Journal of Church and State|volume=32|issue=3|pages=527–547|issn=0021-969X}}</ref>It is the paratrooper shrine

'''Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine''' is a shrine located in {{ill|10=ja|11=川南町|Kawaminami, Miyazaki|lt=Kawaminami Town|4=en|12=draft}} , {{ill|10=ja|11=児湯郡|Koyu District, Miyazaki|lt=Koyu|4=en|12=draft}} [[Miyazaki Prefecture|District, Miyazaki Prefecture]]. The temple grounds are also known as the birthplace of the Army {{ill|5=Fallschirmtruppe_(Kaiserlich_Japanisches_Heer)|10=ja|11=挺進連隊|Airborne Parachute Unit|4=de|12=draft}}.


