[[simple:user:immanuelle/Kawanami Gokoku Shrine]]
{{ill|Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine|ja|川南護国神社|1=Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} (川南護国神社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|en}} located in {{ill|Miyazaki Prefecture|en||12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}  {{ill|Japan|en}}.  It is a {{ill|Gokoku Shrines|Gokoku Shrine|en|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}, or a shrine dedicated to war dead.{{Efn|Is this {{ill|WP:BLUE|en}} considering that the shrine is literally named as such?}} Such shrines were made to serve to enshrine the war dead, and they were all considered "branches" of {{ill|Yasukuni Shrine|en}}. They were renamed from {{ill|Gokoku Shrines|lt=Shokonsha|en|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} in 1939.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=TAKAYAMA|first=K. PETER|date=1990|title=Enshrinement and Persistency of Japanese Religion|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/23917081|journal=Journal of Church and State|volume=32|issue=3|pages=527–547|issn=0021-969X}}</ref>It is the paratrooper shrine

'''Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine''' is a shrine located in {{ill|10=ja|11=川南町|Kawaminami, Miyazaki|lt=Kawaminami Town|4=en|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} , {{ill|10=ja|11=児湯郡|Koyu District, Miyazaki|lt=Koyu|4=en|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} [[Miyazaki Prefecture|District, Miyazaki Prefecture]]. The temple grounds are also known as the birthplace of the Army {{ill|5=Fallschirmtruppe_(Kaiserlich_Japanisches_Heer)|10=ja|11=挺進連隊|Airborne Parachute Unit|4=de|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}.


== overview ==
[[File:「空挺落下傘部隊発祥之地」碑.jpg|thumb|Monument to the "Birthplace of the Airborne Paratroopers"]]
The shrine enshrines the spirits of 634 war dead from the former Kawaminami village and approximately 12,000 war dead from the Army Raiding Corps.  This is because the Teishin Shrine, which was established within the grounds of the Army Volunteer Training Department, was burned down by the {{ill|10=ja|11=連合国軍最高司令官総司令部|Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers|lt=GHQ|4=en|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} when it occupied Miyazaki after the war, and was therefore enshrined when the Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine was constructed. <ref name=":0" />

In addition, since the temple grounds are the former site of a landing strip for parachute training, a monument marking the birthplace of airborne parachute units has been erected there. Every year, an festival is held by the Memorial Association, attended by the town, surviving families, members of the Airborne Veterans Association and the Airborne Comrades Association, and members of the {{ill|10=ja|11=第1空挺団_(陸上自衛隊)|1st Airborne Brigade (Japan)|4=en|lt=1st Airborne Brigade|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} of the Japan {{ill|10=ja|11=陸上自衛隊|Japan Ground Self-Defense Force|4=en|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}, the successor to the Army Volunteer Corps.

Although it calls itself [[Gokoku Shrines|a Gokoku Shrine]], it is not included in the list of Gokoku Shrines across the country posted on [[Yasukuni Shrine|the Yasukuni Shrine]] website. (See the section on Gokoku Shrine.)


== History ==
{{main|Teishin Shudan|{{ill|5=Fallschirmtruppe_(Kaiserlich_Japanisches_Heer)|10=ja|11=挺進連隊|Airborne Parachute Unit|4=de|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}}}
The following is a summary of the inscription on the "Birthplace of the Airborne Parachute Regiment Monument"  and references <ref name=":0">{{Cite journal|last=英霊にこたえる会|date=1999-3-31|year=1999th year |title=一、老兵は訴える|url=http://www.eireinikotaerukai.com/pdf/tayori/Eireitayori_No34.pdf|journal=英霊にこたえる会たより|volume=34}}</ref>

With the establishment of the Army Airborne Training Department [[1941|in 1941]], the unit was relocated from {{ill|10=ja|11=白城子陸軍飛行学校|the Baicheng Army Flying School|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} in [[Manchuria]] to {{ill|10=ja|11=唐瀬原|Karasehara|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} and {{ill|10=ja|11=新田原|Nyutabaru|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} in Miyazaki Prefecture. At that time, the Army {{ill|10=ja|11=軍馬補充部|Horse Replacement Department|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}'s military horse ranch in Karasehara, Kawaminami village, was converted and used as a parachute landing site.

The assault troops were deployed to combat, and in order to commemorate the heroic spirits who died on the battlefield, '''the Assault Shrine''' was established on the grounds of the Army Volunteer Training Department in Toyohara in [[1944]] (Showa 19). <ref>樺太の豊原（[[ユジノサハリンスク]]）でなく、川南町川南の旧地名。「豊原住宅」などにその名前が残っている。</ref> 


In 1944, the '''{{ill|Teishin Shrine|ja|挺進神社|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}''' was established within the grounds of the {{ill|Airborne Parachute Unit|4=de|5=Fallschirmtruppe_(Kaiserlich_Japanisches_Heer)|10=ja|11=挺進連隊|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} in Kawanami, Miyazaki Prefecture, to honor fallen soldiers. It was destroyed by GHQ after the war.<ref name=":02">{{Cite journal|author=Association to Honor the Spirits of the War Dead|year=1999|date=1999-3-31|title=Veterans Speak Out|url=http://www.eireinikotaerukai.com/pdf/tayori/Eireitayori_No34.pdf|journal=Newsletter to Honor the Spirits|volume=34}}</ref> In 1949, it was enshrined at the newly established {{ill|Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine|ja|川南護国神社|1=Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref name=":02" />


After Japan's defeat in the war, the building of the Army Volunteer Training Unit was used by {{ill|10=ja|11=宮崎師範学校|Miyazaki Normal School|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}, which managed the Teishin Shrine. However, the GHQ, which occupied Miyazaki at the end [[1945|of 1945]], burned down the shrine in the early summer of [[1946]] .

For a time, the altar of Ishikawa Fujinosuke in Karase, Kawaminami village, was borrowed to enshrine the heroic spirits, but [[1949|in 1949]] (Showa 24), when Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine was built to enshrine 634 heroic spirits of Kawaminami village, the heroic spirits of Teishin Shrine were enshrined there together.

[[1956|In 1956]], some of the heroic spirits from Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine were {{ill|10=ja|11=分霊|Bunrei|lt=enshrined|4=en|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}} at {{ill|10=ja|11=高野山|Mount Kōya|4=en|12=draft|12=draft|12=draft}}. <ref name=":0">{{Cite journal|last=英霊にこたえる会|date=1999-3-31|year=1999th year |title=一、老兵は訴える|url=http://www.eireinikotaerukai.com/pdf/tayori/Eireitayori_No34.pdf|journal=英霊にこたえる会たより|volume=34}}</ref>

[[1963|In 1963]], a monument commemorating the birthplace of the Airborne Parachute Regiment was erected. <ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=川南護國神社|url=https://www.asahi-net.or.jp/~un3k-mn/gokoku-kawanami.htm|access-date=2019-12-19|publisher=愛國顕彰ホームページ|和書}}</ref>

[[1991|In 1991]], in order to leave a record of the volunteer corps for future generations, a monument was erected to commemorate “the origins of the enshrinement of over 10,000 heroic spirits of the paratrooper corps at Kawaminami Gokoku Shrine” <ref name=":0">{{Cite journal|last=英霊にこたえる会|date=1999-3-31|year=1999th year |title=一、老兵は訴える|url=http://www.eireinikotaerukai.com/pdf/tayori/Eireitayori_No34.pdf|journal=英霊にこたえる会たより|volume=34}}</ref> <ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=川南護國神社|url=https://www.asahi-net.or.jp/~un3k-mn/gokoku-kawanami.htm|access-date=2019-12-19|publisher=愛國顕彰ホームページ|和書}}</ref> .

== Monuments ==

=== "Birthplace of the Airborne Parachute Corps" Monument ===
{{Quote frame|'''空挺落下傘部隊発祥之地'''<br>花負いて　空射ち征かん　雲染めん　屍悔なく　兵等散るなり<br>昭和三十八年五月建立　　空挺戦友会　空挺同志会　遺族一同|碑文引用出典<ref name=":1" />}}

{{Quote frame|'''Birthplace of the Airborne Parachute Corps'''<br>Carrying flowers, shooting through the skies to advance, dyeing the clouds,<br>No regrets for their corpses, as soldiers scatter and fall.<br>Erected May, Showa 38 (1963) by the Paratrooper Comrades Association, Paratrooper Fellowship Association, and all bereaved families.|Inscription source<ref name=":1" />}}


